{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794397093,"nonce":"nRBLE+0lCRxXbeW/veEM9u9MuJk=","message":"Events are covered by a stream in place of a closure stored in Rust.\n\nWhy not a stored closure. To hold a Haxe function past the call, Rust would have to root it and later unroot it. A handle's value is dropped by the collector's finalizer, and stock HashLink's `hl_remove_root` takes the collector's lock, which the collection running the finalizer already holds. And a worker thread could never call the function at all, being a thread the VM does not know. Both problems come from native code holding and calling something of the host's.\n\nWhat was built instead. `hxn_future::channel` gives a `Sender` and a `Stream`. The library sends from any thread; the host waits and takes, item by item, and calls whatever Haxe function it likes with each. Nothing of the host's is held in Rust. `hxn.Stream` on the Haxe side is an iterator (`for (item in stream)`) and has `listen`, which delivers each item on the calling thread's event loop as `Future.then` does. A stream ends when every sender is dropped or with `fail`, whose failure the reader meets after the items before it; a sender learns its reader is gone because `send` returns false.\n\nTested by seven unit tests and the fixture (`countUp`, an `Emitter` fed from Haxe) on Ash in hybrid, jit and interp modes, Ash wasm, stock HashLink, hxcpp and Emscripten.\n\nLeft open on this issue:\n\n- A callback that must return a value to the library at a time of the library's choosing, off the Haxe thread. Neither lending nor a stream gives that, and nothing so far has needed it.\n- A function kept across calls and called during later calls on the Haxe thread (a comparator held by a container, say). The binding can keep it on the Haxe side and lend it each time.\n- Callback argument and result types beyond `Int`.\n- Streams are unbounded: a library that sends faster than the host reads grows the queue. There is no way yet for Rust `async` code to read a stream.","files":null}]}